```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE)
```

A transformation takes an R function and returns a new function. Transformations
compose freely: the gradient of a compiled function, a compiled gradient, a vectorized
Hessian. This vignette covers all of them.

```{r}
library(jaxR)
```

## How tracing works

A transformation calls your function with *tracers*: stand-ins that record the
operations applied to them. The function must therefore be written with jaxR operations
(`jnp_*`, `lax_*`, the R operators) and must be *pure*: its result depends only on its
arguments, and it has no side effects.

`make_jaxpr()` shows the recorded program.

```{r}
f <- function(x) jnp_sum(jnp_sin(x) * x)
make_jaxpr(f)(as_jax(c(1, 2, 3)))
```

Ordinary R code that does not depend on array *values* runs once at trace time: loops
over a fixed range, `if` on a shape or on a plain R value, calls to other R functions.

```{r}
poly <- function(x, coefs) {
  out <- 0
  for (cf in coefs) out <- out * x + cf     # an R loop, unrolled in the trace
  out
}
make_jaxpr(function(x) poly(x, c(1, -2, 3)))(as_jax(2))
```

R control flow on an array value does not work under tracing, because the tracer has no
value. Use `jnp_where()` or the structured control flow of `lax` (`lax_cond`,
`lax_while_loop`, `lax_scan`; see `vignette("lax", package = "jaxR")`).

```{r}
relu_bad <- function(x) if (as.logical(x > 0)) x else 0 * x
tryCatch(grad(relu_bad)(as_jax(1)), error = function(e) class(e)[1])
relu <- function(x) jnp_where(x > 0, x, 0)
grad(relu)(as_jax(1))
```

## Differentiation

### grad and value_and_grad

`grad(f)` returns the gradient of a scalar valued function with respect to its first
argument.

```{r}
x <- as_jax(c(1, 2, 3))
grad(f)(x)
```

`argnums` selects other arguments (0-based). With several, the result is a list.

```{r}
loss <- function(w, b, x, y) jnp_mean((x %*% w + b - y)^2)
w <- as_jax(c(0.5, -0.5)); b <- as_jax(0.1)
X <- as_jax(matrix(c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6), 3, byrow = TRUE)); y <- as_jax(c(1, 0, 1))
grad(loss)(w, b, X, y)
grad(loss, argnums = c(0L, 1L))(w, b, X, y)
```

Arguments can be nested lists of arrays (*pytrees*); the gradient has the same
structure.

```{r}
loss2 <- function(params, x, y) jnp_mean((x %*% params$w + params$b - y)^2)
grad(loss2)(list(w = w, b = b), X, y)
```

`value_and_grad()` returns the value too, and `has_aux = TRUE` lets the function return
extra data that is not differentiated.

```{r}
value_and_grad(loss)(w, b, X, y)
with_aux <- function(w) {
  r <- X %*% w - y
  list(jnp_mean(r^2), list(residual = r))
}
grad(with_aux, has_aux = TRUE)(w)
```

Gradients nest, so higher derivatives need nothing new.

```{r}
g3 <- grad(grad(grad(function(x) x^4)))
g3(as_jax(2))            # 24 x
```

### Forward mode: jvp

`jvp(f, primals, tangents)` evaluates the function and its directional derivative
(Jacobian times vector) in one pass.

```{r}
jvp(function(x) jnp_sin(x) * x, list(x), list(jnp_ones_like(x)))
```

### Reverse mode: vjp

`vjp(f, ...)` returns the value and a function that maps an output cotangent to the
input cotangents (vector times Jacobian).

```{r}
r <- vjp(function(x) jnp_sin(x) * x, x)
r[[1]]
r[[2]](as_jax(c(1, 0, 0)))
```

Two options of `vjp()` control what is computed and kept. `in_nzs` marks the inputs that
may have a nonzero tangent: the others are treated as constants, and
`vjp_fun$out_nzs` then tells which outputs still depend on a marked input.
`saveable_args` marks the arguments that may be kept for the backward pass. An argument
array that the backward pass needs as it is, but that is marked `FALSE`, is not kept:
`vjp_fun$args_res` shows `NotSaveable()` in its place, and the caller puts the value
back before calling the function (useful when the argument lives elsewhere anyway, so
that it is not held twice). Arguments the backward pass does not need show
`NotNeeded()`.

```{r}
a <- as_jax(2, "float32")
b <- as_jax(3, "float32")
r <- vjp(function(x, y) list(x * y, y), a, b, in_nzs = list(TRUE, FALSE))
r[[2]]$out_nzs
r <- vjp(function(x, y) x * y, a, b, saveable_args = list(TRUE, FALSE))
f <- r[[2]]
f$args_res
f$args_res[[2]] <- b
f(as_jax(1, "float32"))
```

### linearize

`linearize()` evaluates once and returns the linear map of the JVP, which can then be
applied to many tangents without recomputing the function. `linear_transpose()`
transposes a linear function.

```{r}
lin <- linearize(function(x) jnp_sin(x) * x, x)
lin[[2]](as_jax(c(1, 0, 0)))
lt <- linear_transpose(function(v) 3 * v, x)
lt(as_jax(c(1, 1, 1)))
```

### Jacobians and Hessians

`jacfwd()` builds the Jacobian with forward mode (one pass per input), `jacrev()` with
reverse mode (one pass per output); `jacobian()` is `jacrev()`. `hessian()` is forward
over reverse.

```{r}
g <- function(x) jnp_stack(list(x[0] * x[1], jnp_sin(x[2]), jnp_sum(x^2)))
jacfwd(g)(x)
jacrev(g)(x)
hessian(f)(x)
```

### Stopping gradients

`stop_gradient()` treats a value as a constant.

```{r}
grad(function(x) jnp_sum(x * stop_gradient(x)))(x)   # only one factor is differentiated
```

### Integer and boolean inputs

Integers have no tangent space. The cotangent of an integer input has dtype `float0`,
and `grad()` asks for `allow_int = TRUE` to differentiate with respect to one.

```{r}
r <- vjp(function(x, n) x * n, as_jax(c(1, 2)), as_jax(3L))
r[[2]](as_jax(c(1, 1)))
```

### Custom derivatives

`custom_jvp()` and `custom_vjp()` replace the derivative of a function, for numerical
stability or to differentiate through an iterative solver.

```{r}
log1pexp <- custom_jvp(
  function(x) jnp_log1p(jnp_exp(x)),
  jvp = function(primals, tangents) {
    x <- primals[[1]]
    list(log1pexp(x), tangents[[1]] * (1 - 1 / (1 + jnp_exp(x))))
  })
grad(log1pexp)(as_jax(100))     # the naive derivative overflows to NaN
grad(function(x) jnp_log1p(jnp_exp(x)))(as_jax(100))
```

```{r}
clip_gradient <- custom_vjp(
  function(x) x,
  fwd = function(x) list(x, NULL),
  bwd = function(res, g) list(jnp_clip(g, -1, 1)))
grad(function(x) jnp_sum(clip_gradient(x) * 10))(x)
```

`custom_gradient()` is the TensorFlow style shortcut and `lax_custom_root()`,
`lax_custom_linear_solve()` define implicit derivatives of root finders and linear
solvers.

### Checkpointing

Reverse mode stores the intermediate values of the forward pass. `checkpoint()` (alias
`remat()`) trades memory for time: the wrapped function is recomputed during the
backward pass instead of having its intermediates stored. `print_saved_residuals()`
shows what a function stores.

```{r}
layer <- function(x) jnp_sin(jnp_sin(x))
deep <- function(x) jnp_sum(checkpoint(layer)(checkpoint(layer)(x)))
grad(deep)(x)
```

### Taylor mode: higher derivatives in one pass

`experimental_jet_jet()` is the port of `jax.experimental.jet`. `jvp()` pushes a value and
one tangent through a function; `experimental_jet_jet()` pushes a value and a whole
truncated Taylor series, so the derivatives of order 1 to K along a direction come out
of one pass instead of K nested calls of `grad()` or `jvp()`.

The arguments are the function, the list of its arguments (the point of expansion) and,
for each argument, the list of its higher order terms. With the series `(1, 0, 0)` for a
scalar argument the output terms are the first three derivatives:

```{r}
f <- function(x) jnp_sin(x) * jnp_exp(x)
out <- experimental_jet_jet(f, list(0.4), list(list(1, 0, 0)))
out[[1]]              # f(0.4)
out[[2]]              # f'(0.4), f''(0.4), f'''(0.4)
grad(grad(grad(f)))(0.4)
```

A series that is not `(1, 0, ...)` describes a curve through the point, and the result
is the Taylor expansion of the function along that curve (Faà di Bruno's formula). With
`factorial_scaled = FALSE` the terms are Taylor coefficients, the derivatives divided by
the factorial of their order. `experimental_jet_jet()` composes with `jit()` and
`vmap()`. As in JAX there are rules for arithmetic, the elementary functions, linear
structural operations, reductions, products, convolutions and gathers; control flow
primitives have none, and custom derivative rules are ignored (the function itself is
differentiated).

## Vectorization: vmap

`vmap(f)` maps `f` over a leading axis of its arguments without a loop: the batch axis is
pushed through each primitive.

```{r}
dot <- function(a, b) jnp_sum(a * b)
A <- as_jax(matrix(1:6, 2, byrow = TRUE), "float32")
B <- as_jax(matrix(6:1, 2, byrow = TRUE), "float32")
vmap(dot)(A, B)
```

`in_axes` gives the mapped axis of each argument (`NULL` for an argument that is not
mapped) and `out_axes` places the batch axis of the result.

```{r}
vmap(dot, in_axes = list(0L, NULL))(A, as_jax(c(1, 1, 1)))
vmap(dot, in_axes = list(1L, 1L))(A, B)
vmap(function(a) a * 2, out_axes = 1L)(A)
```

A common use is per example gradients: differentiate the loss of one example, then map
over the batch.

```{r}
loss1 <- function(w, x, y) (jnp_sum(x * w) - y)^2
vmap(grad(loss1), in_axes = list(NULL, 0L, 0L))(w, X, y)
```

Nested `vmap` gives all pairs.

```{r}
vmap(function(a) vmap(function(b) dot(a, b))(B))(A)
```

`axis_name` names the mapped axis so that collectives such as `lax_psum()` can reduce over
it from inside the function.

```{r}
vmap(function(v) v / lax_psum(v, "batch"), axis_name = "batch")(as_jax(c(1, 2, 5)))
```

## Compilation: jit

`jit(f)` traces `f` once per combination of argument shapes and dtypes, compiles the
program with XLA and reuses the executable afterwards.

```{r}
selu <- function(x, alpha = 1.67, lambda = 1.05) lambda * jnp_where(x > 0, x, alpha * jnp_exp(x) - alpha)
selu_jit <- jit(selu)
big <- jnp_linspace(-3, 3, 1000000L)
invisible(selu_jit(big))                 # first call compiles
system.time(for (i in 1:20) block_until_ready(selu(big)))["elapsed"]
system.time(for (i in 1:20) block_until_ready(selu_jit(big)))["elapsed"]
```

Arguments that decide the *structure* of the computation (a shape, a flag, a function)
must be static: name them in `static_argnums` or `static_argnames`. The function is
recompiled for each new static value.

```{r}
make <- jit(function(x, n) jnp_reshape(x, c(n, -1L)), static_argnums = 1L)
make(jnp_arange(6), 2L)
make(jnp_arange(6), 3L)
```

A traced value cannot be used where a concrete one is needed; the error says so.

```{r}
bad <- jit(function(x, n) jnp_reshape(x, c(n, -1L)))
tryCatch(bad(jnp_arange(6), 2L), error = function(e) class(e)[1:2])
```

Useful tools around compilation:

* `disable_jit(code)` runs `code` with `jit` turned off, to debug with ordinary R tools;
* `log_compiles(code)` reports each compilation and `explain_cache_misses(code)` explains
  why a function was traced again;
* `clear_caches()` drops the compiled executables;
* `eval_shape(f, ...)` computes the shapes and dtypes of the result without running `f`;
* `lower_text(f, ...)` returns the StableHLO text of the lowered function;
* `ensure_compile_time_eval(code)` evaluates constant operations eagerly during tracing.

```{r}
eval_shape(function(a, b) a %*% b, ShapeDtypeStruct(c(3L, 4L), "float32"), ShapeDtypeStruct(c(4L, 2L), "float32"))
cat(lower_text(function(x) x * 2 + 1, as_jax(c(1, 2))))
```

### Ahead of time compilation, saved executables and the disk cache

`jit(f)$lower(...)` lowers `f` for the given arguments (or `ShapeDtypeStruct` values)
and `$compile()` compiles it; the compiled function only accepts arguments of those
shapes and dtypes. As in `jax.experimental.serialize_executable`, a compiled function
can be saved and loaded by a later R session on the same platform with the same XLA
runtime, without tracing or compiling again.

```{r}
compiled <- jit(function(x) jnp_tanh(x) * 2)$lower(as_jax(c(0, 1)))$compile()
s <- experimental_serialize_executable_serialize(compiled)
path <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(s, path)
s <- readRDS(path)
loaded <- experimental_serialize_executable_deserialize_and_load(s[[1]], s[[2]], s[[3]])
loaded(as_jax(c(0.5, 2)))
```

The persistent compilation cache does the same automatically: with a cache directory
set, every program whose compilation took at least
`jax_persistent_cache_min_compile_time_secs` seconds (default 1) is written there, and
later sessions read it back instead of compiling. `jax_enable_compilation_cache = FALSE`
turns it off. A cached file is native code, so use a directory only you can write to.

```{r}
experimental_compilation_cache_set_cache_dir(file.path(tempdir(), "jax_cache"))
jax_config_update("jax_persistent_cache_min_compile_time_secs", 0)
invisible(jit(function(x) jnp_cos(x) + 1)(as_jax(1)))
length(list.files(file.path(tempdir(), "jax_cache"))) > 0
jax_config_update("jax_compilation_cache_dir", NULL)
jax_config_update("jax_persistent_cache_min_compile_time_secs", 1)
```

## Composition

The transformations are designed to be combined in any order.

```{r}
per_example_hessian <- jit(vmap(hessian(function(x) jnp_sum(x^3))))
per_example_hessian(as_jax(matrix(c(1, 2, 3, 4), 2)))
```

## Summary of the transformation API

| Function | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `grad`, `value_and_grad` | gradient of a scalar function |
| `jvp`, `linearize` | forward mode |
| `vjp`, `linear_transpose` | reverse mode |
| `jacfwd`, `jacrev`, `jacobian`, `hessian` | full derivative matrices |
| `stop_gradient` | block differentiation |
| `custom_jvp`, `custom_vjp`, `custom_gradient` | user defined derivative rules |
| `checkpoint` (`remat`), `checkpoint_policies`, `print_saved_residuals` | memory and time trade-off in reverse mode |
| `experimental_jet_jet` | Taylor mode: derivatives of order 1 to K in one pass |
| `fwd_and_bwd`, `closure_convert`, `linear_call` | lower level building blocks |
| `vmap`, `sequential_vmap`, `custom_vmap` | vectorization and user defined batching rules |
| `jit`, `disable_jit`, `clear_caches`, `make_jaxpr`, `eval_shape`, `lower_text` | compilation and inspection |
| `experimental_serialize_executable_serialize`, `experimental_compilation_cache_set_cache_dir` | saved executables and the disk cache |
| `named_call`, `named_scope` | names in the lowered program |
